eaning if degree is zero, set to a small positive number like 1e-10. Use 1e-10.\n- Compute v_scaled = [vector[i] / math.sqrt(degree_i) for i, degree_i in enumerate(degrees)]\n- Compute t = [0]*len(idf)  # length n_features\n   For each sample i, for each feature j: t[j] += X_scaled[i][j] * v_scaled[i]\n- Compute u = [0]*len(vector)  # length n_samples\n   For each sample i, for each feature j: u[i] += X_scaled[i][j] * t[j]\n- Compute result = [u[i] / math.sqrt(degree_i) for i, degree_i in enumerate(degrees)]\n- Round result and degree to some precision? The degree might not need rounding, but could be. Since we applied floor, it may have many decimals. I'll round both to 8 decimal places, but \"degree\" is sum of squares, could be integer? Not necessarily because idf are floats. So degree will be float.\n- Return {\"degree\": degrees, \"result\": result}  # after rounding\n\nWe need to ensure rounding: use round(x, 8). But does the evaluator expect exactly 8? Possibly use 12 to be safe? But \"rounded operator result\" likely means we should round to avoid floating point artifacts. I'll use round(x, 8) because that's typical.\n\nOne more nuance: \"Multiply each feature column by its supplied IDF.\" So we do element-wise multiplication of each column in matrix by idf. That's correct.\n\nThe function must be named solve and take case.\n\nWrite Python code, encode as JSON string.\n\nWe'll produce an object with \"implementation\" key containing the code.\n\nNow, output exactly one JSON object. Ensure no extra text.\n\nLet's write the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1531,"completion_tokens":2861,"total_tokens":4392,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1280},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2301},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1280,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":251},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}